course: dictionary
lesson: 7265
title: A word on a long journey
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pilgrim is a person who travels a long way, usually to a holy place, for religious reasons. The word has a second meaning too: anyone on a long journey with a purpose can be called a pilgrim.
explain: Context helps you here. «In 1621 the Pilgrims and the Wampanoag people shared a harvest feast.» The capital P and the words about settlers and a feast tell you the Pilgrims are a group of people, not just any travellers.
explain: Ask what would fit in the sentence. Then swap in your meaning, "a person on a long journey with a purpose," and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A pilgrim is a person who travels to a holy place for religious reasons.
fact: Pilgrims travel to places such as Jerusalem, Mecca, Lourdes, Santiago de Compostela and Canterbury.
fact: «The Pilgrims» with a capital P means the English settlers who sailed to America on the Mayflower in 1620.
fact: Pilgrim can also mean anyone on a long journey with a purpose.
fact: Chaucer's Canterbury Tales tells of a group of pilgrims on their way to Canterbury.
exercise: «In 1621 the Pilgrims and the Wampanoag people shared a harvest feast.» Who were the Pilgrims in this sentence? | Animals that lived on the farm | Wampanoag leaders from the south | *English settlers who sailed to America on the Mayflower | A group of children from Canterbury
exercise: Priya's family walked to Lourdes, a holy place, to pray. What is a pilgrim? | A small boat | Someone who sells fruit at the market | *A person who travels to a holy place for religious reasons | A type of bread
exercise: «The Wampanoag had helped the settlers survive by teaching them to grow corn.» Which word means the people who came to live in a new land? | helped | corn | survive | *settlers
test: Jo walks to Santiago de Compostela, a holy place, for religious reasons. What is Jo? | *A pilgrim | A farmer | A settler | A tourist
test: Sam walks for five days to raise money for charity, with a clear purpose. Is Sam a pilgrim in the second meaning? | No, pilgrims only go to holy places | Yes, but only if Sam sails in a ship | No, Sam is a runner | *Yes, anyone on a long journey with a purpose can be a pilgrim
exam: Which sentence uses the Pilgrims as a name for a group of English settlers? | *«The Pilgrims came to America on the Mayflower in 1620.» | «A pilgrim needs a sturdy pair of boots.» | «The pilgrim walked slowly up the hill.» | «The pilgrims prayed at the holy well.»
